Fix entire-sem streaming-ingestion contract bugs (branch sem-streaming-ingestion)

Work only in entire-brain. Reviewing internal/cli/semantic_stream.go + semantic.go against real entire-sem schema-1.1
streaming output surfaced one blocker and one data-loss issue. Verified: a real header currently fails to parse with
json: cannot unmarshal object into Go struct field semanticHeader.completeness of type string, so streaming ingestion
aborts on every real snapshot. Brain's tests pass only because their fixtures hand-write "completeness":"partial" (a
string the provider never emits).

Ground truth (real provider shapes)

- completeness is an object {"languages":{...},"relations":{...}} on both the header and the summary. On the lean
header it is {"languages":null,"relations":null}.
- The completeness level string (ok/degraded/unsafe) lives at stats.completeness_level, not at completeness.
- Symbols carry container_id and body_hash. Relations carry relation_scope, resolution, target_kind, evidence. Files
carry bytes.

Fix 1 (BLOCKER): model completeness as an object

1. internal/cli/semantic.go: change semanticHeader.Completeness from string to json.RawMessage (keep
json:"completeness,omitempty").
2. internal/cli/semantic_stream.go: change semanticSummary.Completeness from string to json.RawMessage.
3. In mergeSemanticSummary, change if s.Completeness != "" to if len(s.Completeness) > 0.
4. Wherever the completeness level is needed, read stats.completeness_level by parsing the Stats RawMessage:
var st struct{ CompletenessLevel string `json:"completeness_level"` }
_ = json.Unmarshal(summary.Stats, &st) // st.CompletenessLevel: "ok"|"degraded"|"unsafe"
4. (semanticCompletenessAxis already derives state from PartialFailures, so the axis needs no change.)
5. Update existing assertions/fixtures that treat completeness as a string:
  - semantic_stream_test.go:~60 (res.summary.Completeness != "partial") and :~286 (source.Completeness) — compare
against the RawMessage/object now.
  - semantic_stream_test.go semanticStreamSummaryFixture() and docs/semantic_brain_plan.md (~line 254/258): replace
"completeness":"partial" with the object shape and move the level into stats.completeness_level.

Fix 2 (silent data loss): semanticRecord re-marshal drops new fields

file/symbol/relation are decoded into semanticRecord and re-marshaled, but the struct omits: symbol container_id +
body_hash; relation relation_scope + resolution + target_kind + evidence; file bytes. (Externals/unknown are passed
verbatim — fine.) Pick one:
- Preferred: add those fields to semanticRecord so the contract round-trips (Evidence []struct{Kind,FilePath string; 
StartLine,EndLine int; Detail string}; ContainerID, BodyHash, RelationScope, Resolution, TargetKind string; Bytes 
int).
- Or: pass file/symbol/relation through verbatim (like externals) after ignore-filtering.
- If you deliberately keep a minimal projection, document which fields are dropped and why.

Fix 3 (optional): expose the profile

streamSemanticSnapshot hardcodes full (args := []string{"sem","snapshot",...}). Now that the header carries
profile/relation_set/skipped_relation_families, consider plumbing --profile fast|syntax-only.

Fixture — save verbatim as internal/cli/testdata/sem_snapshot_stream.ndjson

Real entire-sem output (full profile, --worktree), only repo_root blanked. Exercises the object completeness, field
symbols with container_id, relations with resolution/evidence, an external, and the authoritative summary.

